expand_article($pdo, 'passkeys-vs-passwords-why-your-accounts-are-finally-getting-safer', <<<HTML
<p>Passwords have been the default way to prove who you are online for decades, and they've been a persistent security headache for almost as long. Passkeys are the most serious attempt yet to replace them, and they're quietly showing up across major platforms — your phone, your browser, your favorite apps — often with little explanation of what's actually different. Here's what a passkey is, why it's genuinely more secure rather than just newer, and what changes for you day to day.</p>

<h2>Why passwords are fundamentally the weaker approach</h2>
<p>A password is a shared secret: you know it, and the website's server also stores a version of it (ideally hashed, but not always well). That arrangement creates two structural problems. First, if the website's database is breached, attackers get something they can try to crack or reuse elsewhere, since people frequently reuse passwords across sites. Second, because you have to type or paste the password to prove who you are, you can be tricked into typing it into a fake site that looks real — a phishing page — handing the secret straight to an attacker who then has everything they need to impersonate you.</p>
<p>Every password-strength best practice — length, complexity, uniqueness per site, regular changes — exists specifically to work around these two structural weaknesses. Passkeys don't ask you to follow better habits around a flawed system; they use a fundamentally different mechanism that makes both of those attack categories largely irrelevant.</p>

<h2>How a passkey actually works</h2>
<p>A passkey uses public-key cryptography instead of a shared secret. When you set one up, your device generates a matched pair of keys: a private key that never leaves your device (often protected by your fingerprint, face, or device PIN) and a public key that gets sent to the website. To log in later, the website sends a challenge, and your device uses the private key to sign it — proving you have the private key without ever transmitting it anywhere. The website can verify the signature using the public key it already stored, without ever seeing or storing anything secret.</p>
<p>This design solves both structural password problems at once. There's no shared secret for a database breach to expose — the public key is, by definition, safe to be public and useless to an attacker on its own. And because there's no secret for you to type, there's nothing for a phishing site to trick you into revealing; a passkey is also cryptographically bound to the specific website it was created for, so it simply won't work on a look-alike phishing domain even if you're fooled into visiting one.</p>

<h2>What using a passkey actually feels like</h2>
<p>In practice, logging in with a passkey usually means a fingerprint scan, a face unlock, or a device PIN — the same authentication you probably already use to unlock your phone — rather than typing anything. It's typically faster than typing a password, and there's nothing to forget, since the cryptographic key lives securely on your device (or is synced across your devices through your platform's account, depending on your setup) rather than in your memory.</p>

<h2>What happens if you lose your device</h2>
<p>This is the most common worry, and it's a fair one. Passkey systems from major platforms sync your passkeys securely across your devices tied to your account — similar to how your photos or contacts sync — so losing one device doesn't automatically lock you out if you have another signed into the same account. Most services also maintain backup authentication methods during the transition period, and it's worth checking what recovery options a service offers before relying on passkeys exclusively for anything critical.</p>

<h2>Do you have to switch immediately?</h2>
<p>No — passkeys are being rolled out as an additional, optional sign-in method on most services right now, alongside existing passwords, not a forced replacement. You can adopt them gradually, starting with accounts you care most about protecting, without losing access to services that haven't added passkey support yet. Many people run a hybrid setup for a while: passkeys where available, a password manager generating strong unique passwords everywhere else.</p>

<h2>A sensible way to start</h2>
<ul>
<li>Set up a passkey on your most important accounts first — email, banking, and anything tied to account recovery for other services.</li>
<li>Keep a password manager for services that don't yet support passkeys, and make sure each password is unique rather than reused.</li>
<li>Check what backup or recovery method a service offers before relying on a passkey as your only way in.</li>
<li>Don't panic about losing "the password" — with passkeys, the recovery model is generally tied to your device and account ecosystem rather than a secret you have to remember and protect yourself.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The standard behind the scenes, explained simply</h2>
<p>Passkeys are built on an open industry standard rather than being any single company's proprietary invention, which is a large part of why they're showing up consistently across phones, browsers, and operating systems from different makers rather than being locked to one ecosystem. That standard defines exactly how the challenge-and-signature exchange works between your device and a website, so a passkey created through one compliant platform is recognized correctly by any website that implements the same standard on its side. You don't need to understand the cryptography to use a passkey, but it's worth knowing this is an open, widely reviewed standard rather than a single vendor's private system — that's part of why security researchers broadly consider it a genuine improvement rather than a marketing rebrand of something less secure.</p>

<h2>Passkeys on shared or work devices</h2>
<p>Because a passkey's private key is tied to a specific device (or securely synced within one account's ecosystem), using them sensibly on shared family computers or workplace equipment takes a little more thought than a password did. On a shared personal device, make sure passkeys are tied to your own account profile on that device, not a shared login, so a household member using the same computer can't authenticate as you. On managed work devices, IT departments increasingly support passkeys as part of broader account security policies, often with additional controls over how they're provisioned and revoked if an employee leaves or a device is lost — worth asking your IT team about if you're setting up work accounts.</p>

<h2>What the migration away from passwords will realistically look like</h2>
<p>Nobody should expect passwords to disappear on a fixed date — this is a gradual transition measured in years, not months, and plenty of smaller or legacy websites will likely rely on passwords for a long time simply because updating authentication systems takes real engineering effort. What's realistic to expect is that major platforms you use daily will increasingly default to offering a passkey first, with a password as a fallback rather than the primary option, and that password managers themselves are adapting to store and manage passkeys alongside traditional passwords so the transition feels incremental rather than jarring.</p>

<h2>Getting started this week</h2>
<p>You don't need to overhaul every account at once. Pick your email provider first, since email is usually the recovery path for every other account you own, making it the single highest-value place to add a passkey. From there, work through banking, and any service where a breach would be genuinely costly or embarrassing. The rest can wait until those services roll out passkey support on their own timeline — there's no urgency to chase every account simultaneously.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<p><strong>Are passkeys actually more secure, or just more convenient?</strong> Both — the convenience isn't a tradeoff against security here. Removing the shared secret and the ability to be phished are genuine structural security improvements, not just a nicer user experience layered on top of the same risk.</p>
<p><strong>Can someone steal my passkey the way they might steal a password?</strong> The private key never leaves your device and isn't transmitted during login, so there's no equivalent of a database leak exposing it. The realistic risk shifts to device security — protecting the device itself with a strong lock screen matters more than ever.</p>
<p><strong>What if a website doesn't support passkeys yet?</strong> You'll continue using your existing password for that service. Passkey adoption is happening gradually across different platforms and websites, so a hybrid approach is normal for the foreseeable future.</p>
<p><strong>Do passkeys work the same across every device and browser?</strong> Support is broad across current major platforms and browsers, though exact syncing behavior can differ depending on your specific device ecosystem. Checking your device's account settings will show you what's available.</p>
<p><strong>Is passkey technology controlled by one company?</strong> No — it's built on an open standard implemented independently across many platforms and browsers, which is why it works consistently rather than being locked to a single vendor's ecosystem.</p>
<p><strong>Should I remove my passwords once I set up a passkey?</strong> Not immediately. Keep the password as a fallback until you've confirmed the passkey works reliably across the devices you actually use, and check what account recovery options remain available before fully relying on the passkey alone.</p>
<p><strong>Can I use a passkey on a public or borrowed computer?</strong> Most implementations support a temporary cross-device flow — typically scanning a code with your phone to authenticate a login on a device that doesn't have your passkey stored locally, without ever exposing the key itself to that borrowed machine.</p>
